# FORENSICS: did every external event produce the edge it should have?
#
# The third question, and the only one that catches a trigger or handler which
# silently stopped firing. `verify` asks whether the hardware matches NOW;
# `due` asks whether an edge is overdue; neither can see an event that already
# happened and produced nothing.
#
# The case it was built for: logind slept the machine, lock-on-sleep.service was
# asked to lock it first, the unit died 203/EXEC on a bad ExecStart, and systemd
# reached sleep.target and slept anyway -- a failed Before= oneshot does not
# block the transition. Afterwards nothing was in a wrong state and nothing was
# pending, so only history showed it. It had been happening on EVERY sleep for a
# whole refactor while the unit reported `enabled` and `canonical`.
#
# A FAILED UNIT HERE IS THE SIGNAL. `vigilant audit` exits non-zero when an
# event produced no edge, so the unit goes red and stays visible in
# `systemctl --user status` long after the alert has scrolled past. Do not
# paper that over with SuccessExitStatus.
[Unit]
Description=vigilance: reconcile past events against the edges they should have
After=default.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/vigilant audit
